<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baalbek</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heliopolis (Syria)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baalbek is a town in the Beqaa valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, known in antiquity as Heliopolis. Its Roman temple complex, with the temples of Jupiter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a> and Venus built between the 1st and 3rd centuries, is one of the largest in the Roman world. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the sanctuary was fortified as a citadel, and the town was held by Ayyubid princes and later by the Harfush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baalbek.jpg" alt="Baalbek" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lodo from Moscow, Russia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Bacchus,_Baalbek,_Lebanon_(49890013476).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baalbek lies east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litani-river">Litani River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s Beqaa Valley, about 67 km northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. The hilltop of Tell Baalbek shows signs of almost continual habitation over the last eight to nine thousand years. It was well watered by a stream from the Rās al-ʿAyn spring and, in spring, by meltwater from the Anti-Lebanon. Its position in a fertile valley, on a major watershed and along the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> should have made it wealthy early, yet it is never mentioned in any known Assyrian or Egyptian record, unless under another name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Canaanite period the local temples were devoted chiefly to the Heliopolitan Triad: a male god, Baʿal, his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> and their son Adon. The site of the present Temple of Jupiter was probably the focus of earlier worship. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest in the 330s BCE the city, as Heliopolis, formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> annexed it, and the settlers of the colony Colonia Julia Augusta Felix Heliopolitana were probably veterans of the 5th and 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> under Augustus. Coins of nearly every emperor from Nerva to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> were struck there.</p><p>Muslim forces occupied Baalbek in 634, 636 or 637, according to different accounts, either peacefully or after a defence. The ruined temple complex was fortified as al-Qala', &quot;The Fortress&quot;, but the Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> sacked it with great violence in 748, dismantling it and largely depopulating the town. Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> conquered it in 942. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> under John I sacked and razed it in 974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1516 and gave the sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and local tax concessions to the Harfush family, prominent among the Shiites of the valley. Its decline in the eighteenth century is ascribed to several causes, among them the growing attraction of Zahlé.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> passed through Baalbek on 1 November 1898 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and noted both the splendour of the Roman remains and the poor state of the modern settlement. An archaeological team he sent began work within a month, and Otto Puchstein and his colleagues worked until 1904, producing a carefully researched and fully illustrated series of volumes. They found nothing datable before the Roman occupation, although later excavation under the flagstones of the Great Court found three skeletons and a fragment of Persian pottery of the 6th to 4th centuries BCE.</p><p>The temples have suffered from earthquakes, from iconoclasm and from the reuse of their stone. The Temple of Jupiter still had 27 of its original 58 columns standing in the sixteenth century, nine before the earthquakes of 1759, and six now. The ruins were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baalbek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saladin took <b>Baalbek</b> in 1174 and granted it in 1179 to his nephew Farrukh Shah.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-baalbek">Ayyubid Emirate of Baalbek</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harfush were a Twelver Shia clan who held <b>Baalbek</b> and its district by the early Ottoman period.</blockquote><a href="/state/harfush-emirate">Harfush emirate</a></li></ul></section>
